    "When recent history shows a strong downtrend followed by a single high-value spike (e.g., Example 1 and 2), the model's upward forecast is systematically biased; true outcomes often reverse sharply within 5-7 days, causing significant overshoot.",
    "Model forecasts exhibit extreme mean reversion bias when sentiment history contains mixed or negative signals amidst volatile price action; actual paths tend to be more erratic and less linear than predicted.",
    "In periods of high volatility with fluctuating sentiment (e.g., Example 4 and 5), the model fails to capture immediate reversals after short-term peaks, resulting in forecasts that drift persistently away from true outcomes.",
    "The 30-day horizon is prone to over-smoothing; true outcomes frequently show a 'V-shape' recovery or deep crash within the first two weeks that the linear forecast misses entirely."
